# ~/.ssh/config — LOCAL-ONLY, intentionally NOT managed by chezmoi.
#
# Why: work SSH tooling rewrites ~/.ssh/config on every run, re-inserting its
# own configuration block at the top of the file. Letting chezmoi manage this
# file would fight that tooling forever and leak the work configuration (host
# names, absolute paths) into this public repo. So ~/.ssh/config stays local
# and everything managed is pulled in through the Include below.
#
# Bootstrap on a new machine:
#
#   `chezmoi apply` auto-ensures the `Match all` + `Include ~/.ssh/config.d/*`
#   lines below are present in ~/.ssh/config (idempotent, every apply — see
#   .chezmoiscripts/run_after_08-ensure-ssh-config-include). You no longer need
#   to copy this file by hand; it stays as reference and to enable the OPTIONAL
#   pre-connect hook at the bottom. On a work machine the ensured lines are
#   appended BELOW whatever the work SSH tooling wrote — exactly what's required
#   (see the IMPORTANT note below).
#
# IMPORTANT — the `Match all` before the Include is required. The work tooling
# writes its block as a host-specific `Host <work-hosts>` section, and an
# `Include` that sits inside a non-matching Host block is skipped entirely.
# Without this reset to global scope, the personal config under config.d/*
# would only apply when connecting to those work hosts. (Verified with
# `ssh -G`.)
#
# The work-managed block lands above these lines, e.g.:
#
#   Host <work-hosts>
#   Include /path/to/work_ssh_config

Match all
Include ~/.ssh/config.d/*

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OPTIONAL — pre-connect remote prep + self-healing.
#
# ssh-prepare-connection is the single entry point for every pre-connect prep
# this config needs before a session starts. It is driven ENTIRELY by a header
# block in the loose ~/.ssh/config.d/<devbox>.conf it is handed, so the hook
# line at the bottom of every host's config is identical — all per-host variance
# lives in that host's own header. Header block (a `# ---` fenced comment) — it
# must be the FIRST thing in the file (top-of-file front matter; any descriptive
# comments go below the closing fence, still above `Host`):
#
#   # ---
#   # alias: <devbox>             the ssh target: used to connect AND as the
#   #                             theme color-map key (single source of truth,
#   #                             so the hook line needs no %n)
#   # prepare: all | <step …>     which prep steps to run (default: all);
#   #                             including `gpg` (or `all`) also declares that
#   #                             this host wants the gpg-agent forward
#   # ---
#
# The header is the "front matter": system-onboard preserves everything ABOVE the
# `Host` line and re-renders the block below it from `prepare` (Host/HostName,
# clipboard forward, and — when `prepare` implies gpg — the gpg forward + the
# hook line). So on a system-onboard'd host you edit `prepare` here and let
# `system-onboard update <devbox>` regenerate the mechanical parts; the example
# below is what that render produces (and what to write by hand elsewhere).
#
# Steps (prepare) — some push host-specific artifacts computed from THIS
# machine's state (rather than baking them in), one preps remote state:
#   * gpg — pre-creates the remote gpg-agent socketdir so the FIRST post-reboot
#     connection can forward gpg-agent. Always runs before the rsync steps,
#     since they open the forward-bearing ssh master.
#   * symbols-db — mirrors the glyph/gitmoji picker DB
#     (~/.local/share/fonts/nerd-font/symbols.db), built on THIS desktop/
#     laptop and baking in the renderability of this machine's terminal +
#     font — exactly what draws the glyphs when you SSH into a headless
#     dev-shell. The remote ~/.local/share/fonts/nerd-font/ directory must
#     already exist.
#   * theme — computes the background tint THIS machine's WezTerm paints for
#     <devbox> (the onboarded color / profile default / grey, from the shared
#     tint-palette.toml) and writes it as a loose
#     ~/.config/theme/override.toml on the remote; the remote's theme-apply
#     folds it into the effective palette and the Zellij `system` theme at
#     shell startup, so the zj-hud bar, fzf, dialogs, and file viewers there
#     match the window tint (see docs/theme-unification.md §4.8).
# The rsync steps send deltas only — a near-instant no-op when nothing changed.
#
# Resolve (self-healing) — for an endpoint whose value is remote-derived and
# cannot be written with an ssh token (e.g. the gpg agent-socket path, which
# carries the remote uid + gpgconf's homedir hash). Any line carrying a trailing
# `# @@resolve@@: <template>` marker is self-healing: the template (text after
# the marker) is canonical and its @@<key>@@ placeholders are looked up live and
# rendered into the line's left side on each connect. The line self-declares, so
# there's no separate `resolve:` header list. It is eventually-consistent — a
# rewrite lands for the NEXT connection — and best-effort: a line whose keys
# can't all be resolved is left untouched. Example inside the Host block:
#
#   RemoteForward /run/user/<uid>/gnupg/d.<hash>/S.gpg-agent %d/.config/gnupg/S.gpg-agent.extra  # @@resolve@@: RemoteForward @@gpg-agent-socket@@ %d/.config/gnupg/S.gpg-agent.extra
#
# (The left side must stay a valid RemoteForward spec — ssh rejects a bare
# @@placeholder@@ — so a fresh render uses a pending absolute path that heals on
# first connect.)
#
# This is host-specific, so it stays in this LOCAL file only and never goes back
# to the repo. On a system-onboard'd host, `system-onboard` renders all of this
# from `prepare`; elsewhere, write the front matter + the block below by hand.
#
# Why each piece:
#   * `originalhost` (NOT `host`) — `Match host` matches the target hostname
#     AFTER any `HostName` substitution earlier in the file, so if <devbox>'s
#     `Host` block sets a `HostName` different from the alias (e.g. `Host
#     mac-mini { HostName foo.local }`), `Match host <devbox>` silently never
#     matches — it's comparing the resolved HostName, not the alias.
#     `originalhost` matches the alias as typed on the command line,
#     regardless of any HostName substitution, so it works whether or not the
#     alias equals its resolved HostName. (Verified live: `Match host` never
#     fired for an alias with a differing HostName; switching to
#     `originalhost` fixed it.)
#   * the config-file PATH argument (not %n) — ssh has no token for a config
#     file's own path (see man ssh_config TOKENS), so it's passed literally;
#     each loose file names itself. The script reads the header's `alias:` to
#     learn the target, so the argument is purely "which file describes this
#     connection".
#   * `_SYNCREMOTE` sentinel — each step opens its own ssh to <devbox>, which
#     re-reads this config; the guard stops that second pass from re-running
#     the hook (otherwise: infinite loop). One sentinel covers every step
#     since they all run from this single script invocation.
#   * `; false` — makes this a side-effect-only block that contributes no SSH
#     config (the exec never "matches").
#   * timeouts + </dev/null (inside the script, per rsync call) — a sleeping/
#     unreachable dev-shell can't stall or swallow stdin from your login;
#     worst case you connect with a stale copy.
#
# Run it by hand to test: `ssh-prepare-connection ~/.ssh/config.d/<devbox>.conf`.
#
# Match originalhost <devbox> exec "[ -n \"$_SYNCREMOTE\" ] || _SYNCREMOTE=1 ~/.local/libexec/ssh-prepare-connection ~/.ssh/config.d/<devbox>.conf </dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1; false"
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
